      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVES",
          "content": "To identify the benefits and difficulties of families caring for a dying child at home and to assess the parents' adjustment following their child's death."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHOD",
          "content": "Semi-structured interview and questionnaire."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "The benefits of home versus hospital care included greater freedom, more privacy and less disruption to family life, and that caring for the child was a positive experience for most families. Despite this experience many families expressed many fears and concerns about the symptomatic care of their children and requested more support."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "DISCUSSION",
          "content": "Hospital-based treatment teams have a responsibility to ensure adequate support can be provided before home care is recommended for dying children. The experience of home-based palliative care reported by these parents and their suggestions for change will assist all who care for dying children and to improve services supporting home-based palliative care."
        }
      ]},
